1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:10,000
It's explosive, corrosive, toxic, infectious, radioactive, and just plain nasty.

2
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:16,000
No one wants this stuff in their backyard, but to get to anywhere else, it's got to go down your street.

3
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:22,000
Climb inside the cabs of the hazmat haulers and into the hot zone of a spill.

4
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Take a seat on the real Conair.

5
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:32,000
From remote control big rigs to containers that can really take a beating.

6
00:00:32,001 --> 00:00:36,000
Now, Dangerous Cargo on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Industrial poisons.

8
00:00:52,001 --> 00:00:54,000
Radioactive byproducts.

9
00:00:54,001 --> 00:00:58,040
such things are generally brewed up far from where we live and work.

10
00:00:59,720 --> 00:01:02,420
But on their way to market, or a waste dump,

11
00:01:02,780 --> 00:01:04,660
they go right by where we live and work,

12
00:01:05,060 --> 00:01:07,080
in staggering quantities.

13
00:01:08,120 --> 00:01:09,420
Is hazmat pervasive?

14
00:01:10,120 --> 00:01:12,200
The U.S. Department of Transportation estimates

15
00:01:12,201 --> 00:01:14,900
that there are 800,000 hazmat shipments daily.

16
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:17,140
So, it's out there.

17
00:01:18,020 --> 00:01:20,680
It's ubiquitous. That's the only way to characterize it.

18
00:01:20,780 --> 00:01:21,480
It's everywhere.

19
00:01:21,481 --> 00:01:26,360
Given the numbers, occasional accidents are inevitable.

20
00:01:27,460 --> 00:01:32,840
But of increasing concern are not accidents, but the intentional use of hazmat as a weapon.

21
00:01:34,160 --> 00:01:38,180
Including the unthinkable weapon, high-level nuclear waste.

22
00:01:39,900 --> 00:01:45,500
They're going to be dragging dirty bombs, basically, through every major metropolitan area in the United States.

23
00:01:46,700 --> 00:01:51,140
So if you learned the gritty details of hazmat hauling, would you be reassured?

24
00:01:51,420 --> 00:01:52,940
or even more alarmed.

25
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:56,440
Well, if there's one sure thing in this business,

26
00:01:56,880 --> 00:01:58,380
it's that knowledge is safety.

27
00:01:59,220 --> 00:02:02,240
We say, if you don't know, don't go.

28
00:02:02,340 --> 00:02:03,740
That's a fire service expression,

29
00:02:03,900 --> 00:02:05,700
but it also has to do with hazmat.

30
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:08,500
If you don't know what you're doing, don't do it.

31
00:02:10,620 --> 00:02:13,560
The signs are out there, if you know what to look for.

32
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:17,700
These diamond-shaped warning signs or placards tell the tale.

33
00:02:17,701 --> 00:02:24,700
There are nine categories of hazardous materials, each with an unmistakable symbol.

34
00:02:24,701 --> 00:02:31,700
Like a bomb for explosives, flames for combustibles, skull and crossbones for poisons,

35
00:02:31,701 --> 00:02:38,700
the radiating symbol for nuclear material, and tipped over lab beakers for corrosives.

36
00:02:38,701 --> 00:02:44,700
Here's a trailer that's loaded with a little bit of everything,

37
00:02:44,701 --> 00:02:51,300
From low-level radioactive waste to poisonous and infectious material and large quantities of flammables.

38
00:02:53,280 --> 00:02:56,420
As the trailer doors open, hold your breath.

39
00:02:56,980 --> 00:03:00,660
There's a noxious whiff of pent-up vapors from such things as medical waste.

40
00:03:01,900 --> 00:03:06,640
When the freight was loaded, it was segregated by hazard class according to strict regulations.

41
00:03:07,440 --> 00:03:14,020
An example would be you would not want a corrosive liquid loaded next to a poison material.

42
00:03:14,700 --> 00:03:18,500
Should they co-mingle or get together, you end up with a poison gas.

43
00:03:20,540 --> 00:03:22,080
A last cargo check.

44
00:03:23,960 --> 00:03:27,380
This load's ready for the road, so let's go for a ride.

45
00:03:27,940 --> 00:03:30,580
Please climb aboard. We're ready to start our hazmat trip.

46
00:03:31,360 --> 00:03:32,360
Hope you're not nervous.

47
00:03:37,080 --> 00:03:40,760
From a Southern California terminal, the rig's bound for the East Coast,

48
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:43,060
merging with all the other toxic traffic.

49
00:03:44,700 --> 00:03:51,320
You can't get on the highway without coming in close proximity of some type of hazardous load.

50
00:03:52,320 --> 00:03:54,300
This haul is in expert hands.

51
00:03:54,960 --> 00:03:58,240
Charles Potter has logged more than 2 million miles on big rigs.

52
00:03:59,060 --> 00:04:02,020
He's one of a growing number of college-educated truckers.

53
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:06,820
And keeping up with the ever-changing rules and regulations keeps him in school.

54
00:04:08,280 --> 00:04:12,900
They instruct us on the paperwork, on placarding, labeling, markers.

55
00:04:12,901 --> 00:04:17,900
Every two years we go through and get recertified.

56
00:04:17,901 --> 00:04:23,900
And how potentially dangerous is that witch's brew of chemicals in the back?

57
00:04:23,901 --> 00:04:27,900
A load like this could very easily spark off a fire,

58
00:04:27,901 --> 00:04:34,900
and with all the flammable liquid that's on this trailer, it'd be a large fire.

59
00:04:34,901 --> 00:04:38,900
With so much at stake, little is left to chance.

60
00:04:38,901 --> 00:04:42,900
This rig is equipped with Qualcomm's Omnitrack's tracking system,

61
00:04:42,901 --> 00:04:47,900
a satellite link to both the central command center and the dispatcher's office.

62
00:04:47,901 --> 00:04:52,900
This is the antenna communication unit that goes on the top of the truck.

63
00:04:52,901 --> 00:04:56,900
This is the antenna itself. It's inside the device.

64
00:04:56,901 --> 00:05:05,900
And this antenna is constantly tracking the satellite that allows for two-way messaging and position location reporting on the truck.

65
00:05:05,901 --> 00:05:16,340
This device here goes inside the cab, and this allows the driver to both send and receive messages using the keyboard or pre-formatted messages.

66
00:05:19,280 --> 00:05:23,960
The unit continuously updates the truck's position with global positioning information.

67
00:05:25,020 --> 00:05:28,260
And it does more than just deliver a GPS location.

68
00:05:28,261 --> 00:05:40,565
They can tell what engine speeds the engine's running out, how fast you're running, whether you've over-revved the engine, how long you are running down the road,

69
00:05:40,566 --> 00:05:41,780
numerous things.

70
00:05:41,781 --> 00:05:46,640
The system actually is connected to the vehicle data bus.

71
00:05:46,760 --> 00:05:51,120
Now, that's very complicated for saying that trucks are technical now.

72
00:05:51,780 --> 00:05:54,000
All trucks have that same computer system.

73
00:05:54,001 --> 00:05:59,740
Our system actually ties into that, so we can actually notify dispatch of oil pressure problems,

74
00:05:59,880 --> 00:06:04,540
problems that are going on in the vehicle that you may not even know about through the idiot lights on the dashboard.

75
00:06:06,140 --> 00:06:09,780
But how do truckers feel about Big Brother knowing how fast they're going?

76
00:06:09,781 --> 00:06:14,146
At first I had a little trouble with it. I wasn't sure that I liked it.

77
00:06:14,147 --> 00:06:16,360
I almost felt like I was handcuffed.

78
00:06:17,580 --> 00:06:25,520
Believe me, once you break down out in the middle of nowhere, and this Qualcomm is your only source of communication, you learn to appreciate it.

79
00:06:27,020 --> 00:06:30,040
For the real emergencies, there's a red button on the dash.

80
00:06:31,620 --> 00:06:35,520
This is our panic button. It's also tied into the Qualcomm.

81
00:06:35,521 --> 00:06:49,180
Should any type of an emergency arise, we can press that button and an alert will go out to both our company and all law enforcement agencies in the area.

82
00:06:50,920 --> 00:06:59,660
Satellite tracking's original purpose was to control mobile inventory, as the nation switched from traditional warehousing to precise on-time delivery.

83
00:06:59,661 --> 00:07:05,680
But with the rise of terrorism, it's taken on a new role.

84
00:07:07,420 --> 00:07:11,200
Since September 11th, there's been an increased concern with security.

85
00:07:11,720 --> 00:07:16,100
So now we're tracking trucks to make sure that we know whether they're containing hazardous materials,

86
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:21,780
where the hazmat is relative to populated places, water supplies, schools.

87
00:07:22,200 --> 00:07:24,960
And that's the importance of tracking hazardous materials now.

88
00:07:24,961 --> 00:07:37,960
If a hazmat shipment breaches an unauthorized area, operators can disable the truck by shutting down the fuel supply from a computer terminal thousands of miles away.

89
00:07:37,961 --> 00:07:42,960
There's also an option to remotely lock trailer doors.

90
00:07:42,961 --> 00:07:50,960
Out of 4 million long-haul trucks in North America, about 300,000 carry the Qualcomm system.

91
00:07:50,961 --> 00:07:54,780
Which ones? Look for the white domes on the top of the cab.

92
00:07:56,620 --> 00:08:00,540
Actually, my kids do that when we're on vacation, is when we're driving, is we look for the white domes.

93
00:08:00,900 --> 00:08:05,520
And as you're driving around America, you will see those white domes on many different types of vehicles.

94
00:08:07,460 --> 00:08:11,500
But even the all-knowing satellites are no substitute for watchful truckers,

95
00:08:11,840 --> 00:08:15,000
who are more than the eyes and ears of the road.

96
00:08:15,700 --> 00:08:19,540
If you have a truck that's someplace, or a truck or a cargo, that's not where it's supposed to be,

97
00:08:19,541 --> 00:08:22,840
when it's not supposed to be there, chances are another driver's going to see it.

98
00:08:23,820 --> 00:08:27,760
Particularly if you're calling munitions or something like that,

99
00:08:28,520 --> 00:08:35,240
you're looking for people that are spending a little too much time watching you

100
00:08:35,241 --> 00:08:39,280
or coming up and asking you questions,

101
00:08:39,281 --> 00:08:43,060
which in the first place we're not allowed to talk about.

102
00:08:46,340 --> 00:08:48,900
And there's plenty drivers can't talk about.

103
00:08:49,540 --> 00:08:55,640
Tri-State Motor Transit routinely hauls munitions and other sensitive materials for the government.

104
00:08:58,640 --> 00:09:03,960
Some of their trucks operate in the highly secret ghost fleet, which is...

105
00:09:03,961 --> 00:09:05,240
Just that, a ghost fleet.

106
00:09:07,840 --> 00:09:17,580
It's a group of people we have that move things that are highly hush-hush.

107
00:09:19,540 --> 00:09:22,860
These classified movements answer only to their government clients.

108
00:09:23,440 --> 00:09:25,220
Not even trucking officials can track them.

109
00:09:26,320 --> 00:09:30,900
We call it the ghost fleet because we do know it's there, but what it does, we don't know.

110
00:09:33,240 --> 00:09:37,320
There's little mystery to this hazmat loan as we finally hop off.

111
00:09:38,320 --> 00:09:42,620
It's been loaded correctly, placarded prominently, driven professionally.

112
00:09:44,460 --> 00:09:47,620
The job is as dangerous as you allow it to be.

113
00:09:47,621 --> 00:09:53,060
as long as you've done everything you can, you're going to have a safe trip.

114
00:09:55,580 --> 00:09:58,420
Unfortunately, this wasn't always the case.

115
00:09:59,320 --> 00:10:03,600
There was a time when things like nitroglycerin and dynamite were handled and hauled

116
00:10:03,601 --> 00:10:05,500
like so many sacks of concrete.

117
00:10:07,580 --> 00:10:09,460
With predictable results.

118
00:10:12,220 --> 00:10:16,340
The cost of satellite tracking equipment is about $2,000 per truck,

119
00:10:16,341 --> 00:10:18,060
plus $50 a month.

120
00:10:18,980 --> 00:10:22,340
Dangerous Cargo will return on Modern Marvels.

121
00:10:24,340 --> 00:10:26,640
Hazmat's heavy lifting is done by rail.

122
00:10:27,480 --> 00:10:30,800
A railroad tank car can hold more than 30,000 gallons,

123
00:10:31,360 --> 00:10:33,280
or three times as much as a tanker truck,

124
00:10:34,220 --> 00:10:37,100
making for a toxic ocean on steel wheels.

125
00:10:38,300 --> 00:10:41,780
Because there's 225,000 or more tank cars

126
00:10:41,781 --> 00:10:43,280
operating in the United States,

127
00:10:43,400 --> 00:10:46,260
at any given time, maybe a third to a half of them

128
00:10:46,261 --> 00:10:53,260
are out there moving somewhere delivering product so it's a very very big part of our hazmat economy

129
00:10:53,261 --> 00:11:02,500
how are all these big tin cans put together with acres of steel and blizzards of welding sparks

130
00:11:02,501 --> 00:11:09,040
steel up to one and three-eighths inches thick and 12 feet wide is rolled into a ring

131
00:11:09,041 --> 00:11:15,340
long steel fingers called mandrel arms expand within the cylinder to make it perfectly round

132
00:11:16,260 --> 00:11:23,640
A 12,000-ton cold-forming press, the world's largest, punches out the hubcap-shaped tank

133
00:11:23,641 --> 00:11:26,100
heads.

134
00:11:26,101 --> 00:11:31,340
Heads are positioned by the 30,000-pound pull of a vacuum pad, and the whole assembly, or

135
00:11:31,341 --> 00:11:35,380
tub, is fused together by laser-guided welders.

136
00:11:35,381 --> 00:11:42,580
The tub is wrapped in insulation, then jacketed in a second layer of steel.

137
00:11:42,581 --> 00:11:45,620
The axle assembly is called a truck.

138
00:11:45,621 --> 00:11:52,580
One final piece of business, stenciling, and it's ready to roll.

139
00:11:52,581 --> 00:11:57,080
Today's massively strong tank cars are a far cry from the way we used to ship dangerous

140
00:11:57,081 --> 00:11:58,080
cargo.

141
00:11:58,081 --> 00:12:03,740
There's a famous statement by the chemist of the Pennsylvania Railroad, we give more

142
00:12:03,741 --> 00:12:08,800
attention to shipping a carload of horses than we do to a carload of dynamite, which

143
00:12:08,801 --> 00:12:14,660
I think tells you probably all you want to know.

144
00:12:14,661 --> 00:12:21,619
was blown, blasted, and dynamited into shape in the 19th century.

145
00:12:21,620 --> 00:12:23,760
Black powder won the

146
00:12:23,761 --> 00:12:30,488
wars and carved out the right-of-way for the transcontinental railroad.

147
00:12:30,489 --> 00:12:31,720
Nitroglycerin

148
00:12:31,721 --> 00:12:37,120
and other high explosives uncorked the oil wells in rich veins of coal and iron that

149
00:12:37,121 --> 00:12:39,440
made America an industrial superpower.

150
00:12:42,340 --> 00:12:45,660
So how do we haul this hair-trigger stuff to the blast zones?

151
00:12:47,020 --> 00:12:51,120
Sometimes, believe it or not, in briefcases aboard passenger trains.

152
00:12:52,020 --> 00:12:57,500
Makers of explosives would send out salesmen, and they would carry samples with them.

153
00:12:57,501 --> 00:13:06,520
And the railroads would discover that someone was in the passenger car with a suitcase with black powder in it or with a high explosive.

154
00:13:07,120 --> 00:13:09,400
And they didn't like that.

155
00:13:11,100 --> 00:13:16,400
But nothing was done about it until downtown San Francisco was blown sky high.

156
00:13:17,500 --> 00:13:24,200
On April 16, 1866, Samuel Knight, superintendent of the Wells Fargo shipping office,

157
00:13:25,260 --> 00:13:29,340
ducked into a back room where a crate was leaking some sort of oil.

158
00:13:30,400 --> 00:13:33,080
The box was marked only as express freight.

159
00:13:33,081 --> 00:13:37,280
so Knight directed a workman to open it up with a hammer and chisel.

160
00:13:40,680 --> 00:13:43,100
The oily stuff was nitroglycerin

161
00:13:43,101 --> 00:13:46,720
which can explode with the slightest shock.

162
00:13:48,760 --> 00:13:51,820
Fourteen people died including Superintendent Knight.

163
00:13:54,320 --> 00:13:57,500
Even in far off Washington D.C. there were repercussions.

164
00:13:58,800 --> 00:14:02,660
Congress soon passed a bill banning explosives from passenger trains and vessels.

165
00:14:03,080 --> 00:14:06,740
placarding came next.

166
00:14:07,720 --> 00:14:11,920
In 1883, the Pennsylvania Railroad directed that cars carrying explosives

167
00:14:11,921 --> 00:14:14,780
were to display the diamond-shaped signs.

168
00:14:15,660 --> 00:14:17,440
There was just one problem.

169
00:14:18,340 --> 00:14:24,060
Anyone who lives in the country will know that a stop sign is actually a target.

170
00:14:24,600 --> 00:14:27,180
And what the Pennsylvania Railroad realized was that

171
00:14:27,181 --> 00:14:31,100
when they would placard their freight cars with signs that said explosives,

172
00:14:31,101 --> 00:14:38,220
All the boys who were out target practicing when the train went by would take a shot at the sign on the car.

173
00:14:40,400 --> 00:14:43,200
But stray bullets were the least of the railroad's worries.

174
00:14:44,560 --> 00:14:46,620
Deadly and costly accidents were common.

175
00:14:47,700 --> 00:14:52,380
Among the worst, a munitions train that blew up in Crestline, Ohio in 1903.

176
00:14:53,180 --> 00:14:59,300
And in 1905, a runaway dynamite car plowed into a passenger train in Harrisburg, Pennsylvania.

177
00:15:01,100 --> 00:15:02,780
All this carnage had to end.

178
00:15:03,320 --> 00:15:08,720
So in 1907, the railroads created a regulatory body called the Bureau of Explosives.

179
00:15:09,900 --> 00:15:12,500
Unlike some special interest groups, it worked.

180
00:15:13,100 --> 00:15:17,000
There's a big reduction after 1907.

181
00:15:17,200 --> 00:15:24,240
So by the time you get to the 1920s, the dangers of shipping explosives have really receded.

182
00:15:24,560 --> 00:15:28,020
In about 15 years, they've really solved this problem.

183
00:15:28,021 --> 00:15:33,020
But one problem was succeeded by another.

184
00:15:33,021 --> 00:15:38,020
The rise of the chemical and petrochemical industries in the early 20th century

185
00:15:38,021 --> 00:15:42,020
put tons of explosive liquids on the move.

186
00:15:42,021 --> 00:15:45,020
Chief among them? Gasoline.

187
00:15:45,021 --> 00:15:49,020
The explosive properties of gas, though ideal for your car engine,

188
00:15:49,021 --> 00:15:52,020
make it dangerous to haul.

189
00:15:52,021 --> 00:15:57,020
On a hot summer day in 1915, in a quiet Oklahoma train yard,

190
00:15:57,021 --> 00:15:59,420
A worker found out how dangerous.

191
00:16:00,540 --> 00:16:03,000
He took the lid off the tank car.

192
00:16:03,400 --> 00:16:07,400
Well, that's like opening a beer bottle that you've shaken up.

193
00:16:07,620 --> 00:16:10,300
And it went all over the yard.

194
00:16:11,240 --> 00:16:13,840
And the oil lights set off a tank car full of this.

195
00:16:13,900 --> 00:16:19,640
And if you look at a picture of Ardmore, Oklahoma, right after the blast, it's devastated.

196
00:16:19,641 --> 00:16:30,700
What caused the explosion, like this refinery fire, was gasoline's tendency to expand and build up pressure.

197
00:16:33,600 --> 00:16:38,400
Tank cars were crowned with distinctive domes that provided expansion room for gas.

198
00:16:40,940 --> 00:16:45,500
Later on, shippers found it was more economical to only partially fill tank cars.

199
00:16:46,260 --> 00:16:51,220
The domes were gradually phased out, giving way to the streamlined cylindrical forms of today.

200
00:16:54,660 --> 00:16:59,780
By the 1960s, tank cars were safe, but not the tracks below.

201
00:17:00,720 --> 00:17:05,480
The reason for this is that in the 1960s, the railroads are deteriorating.

202
00:17:06,520 --> 00:17:10,620
And their roadbed is deteriorating and their equipment is deteriorating.

203
00:17:10,621 --> 00:17:16,000
So what you begin to see in the 1960s is this system doesn't work anymore.

204
00:17:16,200 --> 00:17:19,620
And it doesn't work because the railroads can't afford to maintain their roadbed.

205
00:17:20,780 --> 00:17:23,860
This recipe for disaster had yet another ingredient.

206
00:17:25,140 --> 00:17:25,840
War.

207
00:17:27,760 --> 00:17:31,460
Munitions bound for Vietnam were rolling over the dilapidated railroads.

208
00:17:33,080 --> 00:17:37,040
There were a number of accidents, including this one in 1973.

209
00:17:37,041 --> 00:17:43,360
A boxcar caught fire from an unknown cause in the Roseville, California yards near Sacramento.

210
00:17:45,200 --> 00:17:49,580
What might have been a small localized fire quickly became a regional emergency

211
00:17:49,581 --> 00:17:52,220
because the car was full of bombs.

212
00:17:53,940 --> 00:17:57,500
Fourteen boxcars detonated in a spectacular chain reaction.

213
00:17:58,360 --> 00:18:00,780
Flaming shrapnel was thrown into nearby neighborhoods.

214
00:18:03,200 --> 00:18:06,600
At the time of Roseville, a revolution was underway.

215
00:18:07,040 --> 00:18:13,900
In 1970, both the Environmental Protection Agency and the Occupational Safety and Health Administration were founded.

216
00:18:16,720 --> 00:18:24,080
To the EPA and OSHA, it was time for the government to stop playing second fiddle to the railroads and their Bureau of Explosives.

217
00:18:25,020 --> 00:18:29,660
And they say, oh, this is unconstitutional, letting the Bureau of Explosives do this kind of thing.

218
00:18:29,661 --> 00:18:37,340
So it becomes much more centralized in the Department of Transportation and the Federal Railroad Administration roughly about 1970.

219
00:18:39,100 --> 00:18:44,400
The story of hazmat hauling has mirrored the last century and half of American history.

220
00:18:45,280 --> 00:18:47,820
The rise and fall of the Great Railroads.

221
00:18:49,240 --> 00:18:50,980
The coming of big government.

222
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,300
The advent of the environmental movement.

223
00:18:55,480 --> 00:18:57,680
An explosive history, to be sure.

224
00:18:59,660 --> 00:19:03,860
Here are some fiery collisions that are no accidents.

225
00:19:04,660 --> 00:19:07,940
They're staged to test the world's strongest transportation container.

226
00:19:08,740 --> 00:19:14,540
It had better be, because it carries the most dangerous cargo of all, high-level nuclear waste.

227
