00:37:03,020 --> 00:37:10,880
Perhaps because their schedules aren't posted, the airplanes aren't marked, and they seldom lose any luggage, let alone a passenger.

461
00:37:12,840 --> 00:37:16,860
Coroner's perfect record isn't matched by the other movers of dangerous cargo,

462
00:37:17,560 --> 00:37:21,760
which is why those rubber hazmat suits are quickly coming into fashion.

463
00:37:23,500 --> 00:37:27,120
In the critical hours after the September 11th terrorist attacks,

464
00:37:27,121 --> 00:37:32,660
J-PATS was one of the very few air services allowed to fly, aside from military aircraft.

465
00:37:33,420 --> 00:37:36,000
Dangerous Cargo will return on Modern Marvelous.

466
00:37:38,500 --> 00:37:43,460
Train wrecks, rollovers, spills, they happen.

467
00:37:43,920 --> 00:37:46,980
Despite training and preparations, spills do happen.

468
00:37:47,460 --> 00:37:55,180
And when they do happen, it's important to respond quickly and decisively and to have the proper resources ready to go.

469
00:37:55,181 --> 00:38:02,640
When the trucker's panic button is hit or 911 dialed, the so-called first responders converge.

470
00:38:03,460 --> 00:38:04,780
Local police or firefighters.

471
00:38:05,980 --> 00:38:13,400
At that point, we decide whether we're going to be very aggressive and offensive in trying to handle the emergency or defensive

472
00:38:13,401 --> 00:38:18,040
and stay back and possibly just as simple as wait for a cleanup crew to come in and take care of it.

473
00:38:19,560 --> 00:38:23,360
What determines that response is the answer to an all-important question.

474
00:38:23,361 --> 00:38:27,620
The very first thing the responder wants to know is, what is this stuff?

475
00:38:28,780 --> 00:38:32,520
One source of right now information is a chemical industry hotline.

476
00:38:33,480 --> 00:38:34,920
Step into its inner sanctum.

477
00:38:36,620 --> 00:38:41,420
You're in the Chemtrak Emergency Center, and this is a 24-hour emergency center

478
00:38:41,421 --> 00:38:47,500
that provides received calls involving chemical spills, hazardous materials, accidents.

479
00:38:48,380 --> 00:38:50,020
Chemtrak Mike speaking. How can I help you?

480
00:38:51,020 --> 00:38:52,180
What's the paint car number?

481
00:38:53,360 --> 00:38:58,360
Chemtrek is wired into the nationwide chemical shipping network.

482
00:38:58,361 --> 00:39:04,360
Operators can match a tank car number to a bill of lading, instantly identifying its cargo.

483
00:39:04,361 --> 00:39:11,360
We get information quickly, nobody gets hurt, and everybody goes home.

484
00:39:13,360 --> 00:39:19,360
Back at the scene of a spill, a hot zone is identified, into which no one may enter without protective gear.

485
00:39:19,361 --> 00:39:25,480
A transition area called a warm zone is staked out for equipment staging and decontamination.

486
00:39:26,600 --> 00:39:31,540
One of the most frightening emergency scenarios is a large-scale chlorine gas leak.

487
00:39:32,720 --> 00:39:36,300
The familiar household chemical chlorine can be deadly when undiluted.

488
00:39:38,040 --> 00:39:47,040
Chlorine gas that was used in World War I as a poison gas chemical is routinely shipped on 90-ton rail cars.

489
00:39:47,041 --> 00:39:54,760
Any accident involving that one rail car could devastate a community 20 miles away.

490
00:39:57,140 --> 00:39:59,300
Fortunately, this is only a drill.

491
00:40:01,380 --> 00:40:05,580
Inhalation dangers such as chlorine gas get the full-blown hazmat treatment.

492
00:40:06,400 --> 00:40:11,380
With Level A protective suits and their SCBA, or scuba-type breathing apparatus.

493
00:40:11,381 --> 00:40:18,000
First we put on our STBA, then we don our suit.

494
00:40:18,001 --> 00:40:21,760
The suit is made of poly-unistane rubber.

495
00:40:21,761 --> 00:40:24,080
We have on three-layer gloves.

496
00:40:24,081 --> 00:40:28,300
We have boots that go over the booty that's on the suit.

497
00:40:28,301 --> 00:40:36,880
Once we're ready to go in, we'll don our masks, zip up the suit, and proceed into the incident.

498
00:40:36,881 --> 00:40:43,740
Inside the hot zone, the real danger spots are sniffed out with high-tech sensors.

499
00:40:44,380 --> 00:40:47,040
We have organic vapor analyzers.

500
00:40:47,380 --> 00:40:51,740
We have combustible gas indicators, toxic vapor analyzers and things like that.

501
00:40:51,860 --> 00:40:56,320
Basically, anything hopefully that we can encounter, we have some way to detect it.

502
00:40:57,860 --> 00:41:03,680
The first responder's role is containment, securing the area and, if possible, stopping the leak.

503
00:41:03,681 --> 00:41:10,680
In this case, Hazmat Techs position a specially designed chlorine cap over the leaking valve.

504
00:41:10,681 --> 00:41:14,680
The fire department's job is now mostly done.

505
00:41:14,681 --> 00:41:17,680
Yet the contaminated area must be cleaned up.

506
00:41:17,681 --> 00:41:19,680
And that's when yet another hotline may buzz.

507
00:41:19,681 --> 00:41:21,680
At the spill center.

508
00:41:21,681 --> 00:41:25,680
Because if you're a trucking company driving from east coast to west coast,

509
00:41:25,681 --> 00:41:27,680
the spill could happen anywhere.

510
00:41:27,681 --> 00:41:31,680
Chances are you're not going to know who's available, who is qualified,

511
00:41:31,681 --> 00:41:36,460
who has the right equipment and materials right now to respond to that release.

512
00:41:37,000 --> 00:41:39,904
There are about 3,000 companies throughout the U.S.

513
00:41:39,905 --> 00:41:41,840
that specialize in hazmat cleanup.

514
00:41:43,120 --> 00:41:46,000
What do they use to sop up a solid, toxic mess?

515
00:41:46,880 --> 00:41:51,120
Certain forms of naturally absorbent clay, which you know as kitty litter.

516
00:41:52,440 --> 00:41:57,400
Sorbent materials, kitty litter is common in the industrial setting,

517
00:41:57,401 --> 00:42:05,060
or smart kitty litter, kitty litter that has an affinity for a particular chemical or a particular type of material.

518
00:42:06,640 --> 00:42:10,220
And finally, someone has to foot a rather sizable bill.

519
00:42:10,940 --> 00:42:17,240
The average amount released is 104 gallons, and the average cost to clean that up nationwide right now is about $9,800.

520
00:42:18,060 --> 00:42:26,560
When it comes to bulk releases, releases from tank cars, the costs are significantly higher, oftentimes $200,000 and up.

521
00:42:27,400 --> 00:42:32,660
A unique American law fixes responsibility for cleanup costs.

522
00:42:33,200 --> 00:42:35,180
It's not who or what caused the accident.

523
00:42:35,620 --> 00:42:38,380
If you were carrying the stuff, you clean it up.

524
00:42:39,120 --> 00:42:41,780
So it doesn't matter that you didn't mean to have the spill.

525
00:42:42,020 --> 00:42:44,880
If you had care, custody, and control of the material,

526
00:42:45,460 --> 00:42:49,900
if it's your chemical and it spills, as a matter of law, you are responsible.

527
00:42:50,400 --> 00:42:54,100
So that's very different from, say, medical malpractice, where there's negligence.

528
00:42:54,101 --> 00:42:58,760
There are some 60,000 reported spills each year in the U.S.

529
00:42:59,760 --> 00:43:02,840
So if you're looking for job security in a growing industry,

530
00:43:03,520 --> 00:43:07,620
consider trading in that business suit for a Level A hazmat suit.

531
00:43:09,600 --> 00:43:13,860
800,000 daily shipments, 60,000 yearly spills.

532
00:43:14,520 --> 00:43:15,680
What does it all add up to?

533
00:43:16,680 --> 00:43:20,060
A very, very large percentage of hazardous material shipments

534
00:43:20,061 --> 00:43:25,640
go from point A to point B, unnoticed, undetected, without any trouble of any kind.

535
00:43:25,960 --> 00:43:30,300
In fact, the majority of those 800,000 shipments a day are transported safely, without incident.

536
00:43:31,060 --> 00:43:36,180
I was quoted in the Washington Post a few years ago in an article called Bombs on the Beltway,

537
00:43:36,181 --> 00:43:42,060
where I pointed out that particular year, almost four times more people were killed in skateboard accidents

538
00:43:42,061 --> 00:43:44,140
than all hazmat transportation in the United States.

539
00:43:45,140 --> 00:43:47,940
And that got a lot of hoots, but a lot of people checked it. He says, he's right.

540
00:43:50,060 --> 00:43:56,840
Over 150 years, we've progressed from buckboard wagons to super containers that can withstand the impact from a locomotive.

541
00:43:59,340 --> 00:44:01,220
A bumpy ride at times.

542
00:44:02,100 --> 00:44:03,700
As it will be again.

543
00:44:04,880 --> 00:44:08,400
Because this is one long haul that never ends.

